         Shared Energy Storage Systems (ESSs) installed at transmission--distribution interface nodes can support both Transmission System Operators (TSOs) and Distribution System Operators (DSOs). Their planning must account for coordinated operation under uncertainty and battery degradation.
-        This paper proposes a two-stage stochastic framework for shared battery ESS siting, sizing and installation timing under investment-cost uncertainty. A derivative-free search explores discrete investment plans, and an Alternating Direction Method of Multipliers (ADMM)-based coordination procedure evaluates their operation under load, renewable-generation and market-price uncertainty. Battery degradation links storage dispatch with available energy capacity over the planning horizon, and numerical acceptance criteria assess the stability of operating-cost comparisons.
+        This paper proposes a degradation-aware framework for the siting, sizing and installation year of shared lithium-ion battery ESSs, formulated as a two-stage stochastic program in which one scenario-independent plan is evaluated under coordinated operation; uncertainty in future converter and battery investment costs enters as probability-weighted cost trajectories. A derivative-free search explores discrete investment plans in a single operating scenario, an Alternating Direction Method of Multipliers (ADMM)-based coordination procedure evaluates their operation, and fixed plans are then re-evaluated under load, renewable-generation and market-price scenarios. Battery degradation links storage dispatch with available energy capacity over the planning horizon, and numerical acceptance criteria assess the stability of operating-cost comparisons.
         The framework is evaluated on an integrated 108-bus transmission--distribution test system over a 15-year horizon. Without shared storage, coordination reduces discounted operating costs by 12.2\% relative to a price-taker benchmark that prohibits reverse active-power flow from distribution to transmission. Under the baseline assumptions, none of the evaluated storage plans recovers its investment cost. For a fixed reference 0.25~MVA/1~MWh unit, baseline ageing yields an investment shortfall of 64.4~k\euro{}, while the margin without ageing is within numerical resolution of break-even. The unit achieves positive investment margins at 1.75 and 2 times the base flexibility price. Its estimated margin remains negative in the $3 \times 3$ multi-scenario evaluation.
     }
